14mL of one medicine + 3 times that amount of the second medicine. First, find the amount of the second medicine. The amount of the second medicine is 3 times the amount of the first medicine, which is 14 mL. So we calculate 3 * 14 mL = 42 mL. Then, find the total amount of medicine for one dose. One dose contains 14 mL of the first medicine and 42 mL of the second medicine. So the total amount of medicine for one dose is 14 mL + 42 mL = 56 mL. Finally, find the total amount of medicine for 8 doses. Since one dose contains 56 mL of medicine, 8 doses will contain 8 * 56 mL = 448 mL.

So the answer is 448.
\boxed{448}

